1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,859
How did America catch Stalin's atomic

2
00:00:02,879 --> 00:00:03,228
spies?

3
00:00:03,698 --> 00:00:05,360
Partly, by reading their mail.

4
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:07,327
This is one of the telegrams.

5
00:00:07,897 --> 00:00:11,138
In November 1944, the KGB in New York sent

6
00:00:11,158 --> 00:00:14,399
Moscow a coded message about a 19-year-old

7
00:00:14,419 --> 00:00:15,874
American physicist.

8
00:00:16,444 --> 00:00:17,472
Theodore Hall.

9
00:00:18,054 --> 00:00:19,354
The son of a furrier.

10
00:00:19,936 --> 00:00:21,396
A graduate of Harvard.

11
00:00:21,966 --> 00:00:24,218
He was in charge of a group at a place the

12
00:00:24,238 --> 00:00:25,516
telegram calls Camp Two.

13
00:00:25,986 --> 00:00:27,931
The translators' notes identify it.

14
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:29,344
Los Alamos.

15
00:00:29,914 --> 00:00:32,126
And he had handed over a report about the

16
00:00:32,146 --> 00:00:34,414
camp, naming the key personnel employed on

17
00:00:34,434 --> 00:00:34,904
ENORMOUS.

18
00:00:35,374 --> 00:00:37,872
The KGB's cover name for the Manhattan

19
00:00:37,892 --> 00:00:38,402
Project.

20
00:00:38,972 --> 00:00:41,297
The Soviet system, the Army's historians

21
00:00:41,317 --> 00:00:43,701
write, was widely considered unbreakable.

22
00:00:44,171 --> 00:00:46,403
U.S. Army codebreakers broke into it

23
00:00:46,423 --> 00:00:46,841
anyway.

24
00:00:47,411 --> 00:00:48,449
This is VENONA.

25
00:00:48,919 --> 00:00:51,317
How Army codebreakers helped expose the

26
00:00:51,337 --> 00:00:53,611
spies inside the atomic bomb project.

27
00:00:54,081 --> 00:00:56,468
And why the 19-year-old in this telegram

28
00:00:56,488 --> 00:00:57,551
was never charged.

29
00:00:58,121 --> 00:00:59,394
Let's open the file.

30
00:01:05,721 --> 00:01:07,991
It began with stacks of paper nobody could

31
00:01:08,011 --> 00:01:08,263
read.

32
00:01:08,884 --> 00:01:11,506
As early as 1939, according to the Army's

33
00:01:11,526 --> 00:01:14,149
historians, the Army had begun collecting

34
00:01:14,169 --> 00:01:16,663
encrypted Soviet telegrams sent between

35
00:01:16,683 --> 00:01:19,370
Moscow and its intelligence offices in New

36
00:01:19,390 --> 00:01:21,625
York, Washington and other American

37
00:01:21,645 --> 00:01:22,077
cities.

38
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:24,875
As they came in, they were put in storage

39
00:01:24,895 --> 00:01:26,411
at Arlington Hall, Virginia.

40
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:29,301
On February 1, 1943, Colonel Carter

41
00:01:29,321 --> 00:01:31,306
Clarke, of the Army's Military

42
00:01:31,326 --> 00:01:33,647
Intelligence Division, ordered them

43
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:34,248
examined.

44
00:01:34,718 --> 00:01:36,662
He feared they would reveal that the

45
00:01:36,682 --> 00:01:38,735
Soviet Union and Germany were secretly

46
00:01:38,755 --> 00:01:40,316
negotiating a separate peace.

47
00:01:40,886 --> 00:01:42,884
The job went first to Gene Grabeel.

48
00:01:43,354 --> 00:01:45,679
She was 23, a schoolteacher who had been

49
00:01:45,699 --> 00:01:47,379
hired just six weeks earlier.

50
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:50,235
The traffic, the Army found, used five

51
00:01:50,255 --> 00:01:51,692
separate Soviet systems.

52
00:01:52,262 --> 00:01:54,445
The most valuable, the NSA says, would

53
00:01:54,465 --> 00:01:56,879
turn out to be the secret agents' traffic.

54
00:01:57,349 --> 00:02:00,051
The KGB and the two military services.

55
00:02:00,971 --> 00:02:02,721
The system had been built so that this

56
00:02:02,741 --> 00:02:03,652
could never be read.

57
00:02:04,272 --> 00:02:06,275
A clerk looked up each word in a code

58
00:02:06,295 --> 00:02:08,406
book, which turned it into a four-digit

59
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:08,734
group.

60
00:02:09,204 --> 00:02:11,547
The numbers were converted into five-digit

61
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:11,997
figures.

62
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:14,797
Then a second set of numbers was added on

63
00:02:14,817 --> 00:02:15,026
top.

64
00:02:15,496 --> 00:02:16,097
The key.

65
00:02:16,667 --> 00:02:18,575
The key came from a one-time pad.

66
00:02:19,045 --> 00:02:21,377
Pages of random numbers, each page never

67
00:02:21,397 --> 00:02:22,376
to be used again.

68
00:02:22,946 --> 00:02:24,502
But the pads were used again.

69
00:02:25,084 --> 00:02:27,992
Under wartime pressure, according to an

70
00:02:28,012 --> 00:02:31,070
NSA history, the Soviet factory that made

71
00:02:31,090 --> 00:02:34,222
the KGB's pads produced about 35,000 pages

72
00:02:34,242 --> 00:02:35,498
of duplicate key.

73
00:02:36,069 --> 00:02:38,305
Duplicate pages meant that some messages

74
00:02:38,325 --> 00:02:39,433
shared the same key.

75
00:02:39,903 --> 00:02:42,526
In October 1943, a team under Lieutenant

76
00:02:42,546 --> 00:02:45,037
Richard Hallock found the first clues.

77
00:02:45,607 --> 00:02:48,546
In 1944, the NSA says, Genevieve Feinstein

78
00:02:48,566 --> 00:02:51,153
found a way to spot the messages that

79
00:02:51,173 --> 00:02:52,069
shared a key.

80
00:02:52,539 --> 00:02:54,951
According to the Army's historians, she

81
00:02:54,971 --> 00:02:57,259
had made the first break into Japan's

82
00:02:57,279 --> 00:02:59,380
PURPLE diplomatic cipher, in 1940.

83
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:02,103
Even then, the work was painfully slow.

84
00:03:02,573 --> 00:03:05,523
Of the many thousands of messages sent

85
00:03:05,543 --> 00:03:08,415
from 1940 to 1948, the NSA says, only

86
00:03:08,435 --> 00:03:11,697
about 3,000 were ever even partially read.

87
00:03:12,617 --> 00:03:14,772
The man who made the messages talk was a

88
00:03:14,792 --> 00:03:15,261
linguist.

89
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,222
Meredith Gardner had taught German at a

90
00:03:18,242 --> 00:03:19,372
university in Ohio.

91
00:03:19,842 --> 00:03:22,088
In the Army, he taught himself Japanese in

92
00:03:22,108 --> 00:03:22,789
three months.

93
00:03:23,259 --> 00:03:25,710
After the war, he taught himself Russian,

94
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,180
and began rebuilding the KGB's code book.

95
00:03:28,751 --> 00:03:32,073
On December 20, 1946, he broke into a KGB

96
00:03:32,093 --> 00:03:35,416
message sent to Moscow two years earlier.

97
00:03:35,986 --> 00:03:38,131
It contained a list of scientists working

98
00:03:38,151 --> 00:03:39,451
on the Manhattan Project.

99
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:43,123
One New York message of December 1944, in

100
00:03:43,143 --> 00:03:46,169
its NSA translation, lists them by name.

101
00:03:46,751 --> 00:03:47,789
Hans Bethe.

102
00:03:48,372 --> 00:03:49,480
Enrico Fermi.

103
00:03:50,062 --> 00:03:51,116
Ernest Lawrence.

104
00:03:51,699 --> 00:03:52,732
Edward Teller.

105
00:03:53,302 --> 00:03:55,585
According to the Army's historians, that

106
00:03:55,605 --> 00:03:58,003
discovery proved the Manhattan Project had

107
00:03:58,023 --> 00:03:59,903
been infiltrated by Soviet spies.

108
00:04:00,473 --> 00:04:03,723
In late summer 1947, the Army cautiously

109
00:04:03,743 --> 00:04:04,786
