110
00:04:05,256 --> 00:04:07,769
And from October 1948, an FBI agent,

111
00:04:07,789 --> 00:04:10,584
Robert Lamphere, worked full time as the

112
00:04:10,604 --> 00:04:13,539
Bureau's liaison to the project, alongside

113
00:04:13,559 --> 00:04:14,102
Gardner.

114
00:04:14,572 --> 00:04:16,986
The decrypts also helped confirm the story

115
00:04:17,006 --> 00:04:19,420
of Elizabeth Bentley, a former courier for

116
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,854
Soviet intelligence, whose confessions had

117
00:04:21,874 --> 00:04:22,608
been doubted.

118
00:04:23,528 --> 00:04:25,772
In 1949, time ran out.

119
00:04:26,391 --> 00:04:29,500
By 1948, the CIA's analysts had a date in

120
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:32,400
mind for the first Soviet atomic bomb.

121
00:04:32,982 --> 00:04:35,242
Earliest possible, mid-1950.

122
00:04:35,825 --> 00:04:38,170
Most probable, mid-1953.

123
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:41,770
According to a CIA history, a report sent

124
00:04:41,790 --> 00:04:44,672
out on August 24, 1949, still gave that

125
00:04:44,692 --> 00:04:45,490
projection.

126
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,612
Five days later, the Soviet Union tested

127
00:04:48,632 --> 00:04:50,026
its first atomic bomb.

128
00:04:50,597 --> 00:04:52,233
An American plane found out.

129
00:04:52,703 --> 00:04:55,161
According to the Air Force, a B-29 flying

130
00:04:55,181 --> 00:04:57,639
between Alaska and Japan, with a particle

131
00:04:57,659 --> 00:04:59,997
sampler on board, picked up debris from

132
00:05:00,017 --> 00:05:00,541
the test.

133
00:05:01,111 --> 00:05:03,074
On September 23, President Truman

134
00:05:03,094 --> 00:05:05,238
announced that the United States had

135
00:05:05,258 --> 00:05:07,522
evidence of an atomic explosion in the

136
00:05:07,542 --> 00:05:08,304
